**Proposition 4.1 (specialization of the credited CP laws).** Fix $d,L>0$,
$v=d/L$ and $0<x<c-d$. For $k_x(t)=x+vt$, constant relative phase,
the reduced live operator and failure CP densities in the ordered basis
$(+,-,2)$ are

$$
\begin{aligned}
S_x(t)&=\frac{x}{x+vt},\qquad
f_x(t)=\frac{vx}{(x+vt)^2},\qquad s_x=S_x(L),\\
K_x&=\operatorname{diag}\left(1,
 \sqrt{\frac{x(c-x-d)}{(x+d)(c-x)}},
 \sqrt{\frac{x}{x+d}}\right),\\
\mathcal I_{x,2}(dt)(\rho)&=f_x(t)|f\rangle\langle2|\rho|2\rangle\langle f|\,dt,\\
\mathcal I_{x,i}(dt)(\rho)&=
 \frac{c}{2(c-x)}f_x(t)|f\rangle\langle-|\rho|-\rangle\langle f|\,dt
 \quad(i=1,3),\\
\mathcal I_{x,0}(dt)&=\mathcal I_{x,4}(dt)=0.
\end{aligned}
\tag{AC.8}
$$

The target on (AC.3) has live block $s_x\rho_{x+d,\beta,m}$ and
five scalar failure densities

$$
(0,c,Z,c,0)f_x(t),
\tag{AC.9}
$$

each with successor $f$. All exact times and original mode names remain.

Proof. These are (OC.55)–(OC.58) of Instrument Boundaries. To verify the
source-family weights rather than assume a waveform-specific example,
the input populations in the reduced basis are $2x,2(c-x),Z$.
The middle CP density in (AC.8) therefore has trace $Zf_x$; each minus
density has trace $[c/(2(c-x))]2(c-x)f_x=cf_x$.
The weights sum to $2c+Z=1$. Thus total failure density is $f_x=-S_x'$
and its mass is $1-s_x$. Multiplying the three amplitudes by $K_x$
gives $\sqrt{s_x}\psi_{x+d,\beta,m}$, with the same phases.
This also checks normalization and the actual live successor. The full
CP maps, not just their traces, are those of the supplier. $\square$

